"""Prepared reference solution for the FlagGems ``rnn_relu`` operator.

Frozen Definition ``definitions/rnn_relu.json`` names
``aten::rnn_relu.input``: an Elman RNN with ReLU activation that returns the
output sequence and the final hidden state for a single, unidirectional layer
without dropout.

The entrypoint ``run`` has the operator's 9-argument positional ABI
(``input, hx, params, has_biases, num_layers, dropout, train, bidirectional,
batch_first``) and delegates to the *native* ATen operator, which is also the
oracle expression used by the frozen master pytest suite
(``tests/test_rnn_relu.py``: ``torch.rnn_relu(...)``).

Why this is a faithful reference and not the candidate:

* it never imports ``flag_gems`` and never calls ``flag_gems.rnn_relu``, so it
  cannot reach the injected export that ``--override`` replaces and cannot
  recurse into itself;
* inputs are only read. ``definitions/rnn_relu.json`` declares
  ``effects.mutates == []`` and ``effects.returns_alias_of == {}``, and the
  native operator honours that: both returned tensors are freshly allocated,
  carry the input dtype and stay on the input device;
* the ``Tensor[] params`` sequence (PyTorch ``_flat_weights`` order
  ``w_ih, w_hh, b_ih, b_hh``) is forwarded unchanged (only re-wrapped into a
  list for the schema), so weight tensors keep their identity, dtype and
  ``requires_grad`` state;
* the op is called eagerly, so autograd still flows through it. The frozen
  master case ``test_rnn_relu_direct_backward`` calls the candidate with
  ``requires_grad`` operands and backpropagates, which this wrapper supports
  unchanged.

Device/precision note (no host execution was performed while preparing this
file): the wrapper is device agnostic and runs on whatever device the operands
live on. On the Ascend (``npu``) target the native ATen RNN kernels are used;
that is the same situation as the frozen dev sibling suite for this family
(``tests/test_rnn_tanh.py``), which enables its reference-as-expected path for
``flag_gems.device == "npu"``. Whether the installed ``torch-npu`` build really
implements ``aten::rnn_relu.input`` is a runtime fact that only the host probe
can confirm; it is recorded as residual risk in the review manifest.
"""

import torch


def run(
    input,
    hx,
    params,
    has_biases=True,
    num_layers=1,
    dropout=0.0,
    train=False,
    bidirectional=False,
    batch_first=False,
):
    """Return ``(output, hy)`` computed by ``aten::rnn_relu.input``.

    ``num_layers``/``bidirectional``/``dropout``/``train`` are forwarded as the
    caller supplied them; the frozen master cases exercise the single-layer,
    unidirectional, ``dropout == 0.0`` configuration only.
    """
    return torch.ops.aten.rnn_relu.input(
        input,
        hx,
        list(params),
        has_biases,
        num_layers,
        dropout,
        train,
        bidirectional,
        batch_first,
    )
